{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "334",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 30s presented with asymptomatic erythematous scaly plaques over the face and proximal extremities. The lesions started as an erythematous papule on the face, progressing to larger plaques within 10 years. There was involvement of the upper respiratory tract, causing palatal perforation and stridor, necessitating a tracheostomy a year ago. History of wheezing and breathlessness. No history of preceding trauma, fever, malaise, joint pain, cough, epistaxis, hemoptysis, or hematuria.\n\nMultiple well-defined brown erythematous scaly plaques with central scarring measuring 2 × 1 to 8 × 7 cm on the face, arm, and thighs. Saddle nose deformity, perforation of the hard palate, and necrotic plaques on the ear with destruction of ear cartilage. Cervical lymphadenopathy: multiple, nontender, discrete nodes, ranging from 1.5 to 2 cm without surface changes. Sensory examination and peripheral nerve examination within normal limits. Respiratory examination normal.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  - Hemogram: anemia (hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL)\n  - Kidney function test: normal\n  - Liver function test: normal\n  - Fasting blood glucose: normal\n  - Urinalysis: normal\n  - Serologic analysis for HIV-1/2, antinuclear antibody, and antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies: negative\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  - Chest radiography: normal\n\n- Pathological Tests:\n  - Punch skin biopsy:\n    Image Description: Infiltrate of foamy histiocytes and pigmented yeasts without multiaxial septation (Fontana-Masson)\n  - Histopathology showed pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia, parakeratosis, dense dermal infiltrate of histiocytes, foamy histiocytes, neutrophils, and multinucleate giant cells along with pigmented hyphae\n  - Fungal skin culture: black yeastlike colonies characteristic of Exophiala spinifera species",
    "root": {
      "label": "Chronic Progressive Multisystem Syndrome with Cutaneous and Respiratory Involvement of Uncertain Aetiology",
      "time_course": "chronic",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.6,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "asymptomatic erythematous scaly plaques over the face and proximal extremities",
        "palatal perforation and stridor necessitating a tracheostomy",
        "history of wheezing and breathlessness",
        "saddle nose deformity",
        "perforation of the hard palate",
        "necrotic plaques on the ear with destruction of ear cartilage",
        "cervical lymphadenopathy"
      ],
      "excluded_candidates": [
        "Infectious disease: although serologic analysis for HIV-1/2, antinuclear antibody, and antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies were negative, the presence of pigmented yeasts on skin biopsy suggests a possible fungal infection",
        "Autoimmune disorder: the absence of antinuclear antibody and antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies makes this less likely, but the multisystem involvement and presence of cervical lymphadenopathy cannot be ignored",
        "Neoplastic disorder: the long-standing nature of the lesions and the presence of destruction of ear cartilage could suggest a neoplastic process, but the lack of systemic symptoms such as weight loss or fever makes this less likely"
      ],
